{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Run inventory-api on node1 as a managed service that serves requests.","status":"satisfied","summary":"inventory-api is enabled and active under systemd, listens on port 8080, and returns successful health and inventory responses. It also returned after reboot with its protection settings intact.","evidence_ids":["cmd-14788d25e7b143e9","cmd-336ae0030a2540ac","cmd-a53219ad41bc4f2a","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Keep inventory-api serving when a memory hog drives the system out of memory, with the kernel stopping the hog rather than inventory-api.","status":"partially_satisfied","summary":"A bounded test directly shows the hog running with oom_score_adj=1000, being kernel OOM-killed, and inventory-api remaining responsive with the same PID and zero restarts. However, MemoryMax=192M caused a memory-cgroup-local OOM while the host still had substantial available memory; this does not directly demonstrate behavior during system-wide OOM or kernel victim selection between the hog and inventory-api.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4e97a2be83144fed","cmd-da89c54174f74923","cmd-2dfe2c6c6ca44737","cmd-3f1ecd9c1f5e4117","cmd-9cbda516fc7e4434"]}],"confidence":0.92,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"All observed mutations had a clear role: creating the service account and implementation, configuring and enabling the unit, running and cleaning up the bounded OOM test, and performing the mandatory restart protocol. No unnecessary mutation was established. The transient failed hog unit and marker were cleaned up, and the final snapshot shows no failed services, healthy package state, and only the task-required listener. Restart evidence is complete: healthy pre-restart state and boot identity, acknowledgement, a different post-restart identity independently confirmed by lifecycle data, and recovered service and inventory responses.","evidence_ids":["cmd-426efa57fafc47d2","cmd-7c2498088874433d","cmd-14788d25e7b143e9","cmd-da89c54174f74923","cmd-974e141255f045ba","cmd-b81ebc83c8844d6b","cmd-e283fc55d55d4aab","cmd-a53219ad41bc4f2a","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},"overall_summary":"The managed inventory-api service is correctly installed, active, responsive, OOM-deprioritized, and reboot-persistent. The demonstrated disruption proves survival of a hog's cgroup-local OOM kill, but not the contract's stronger system-wide out-of-memory scenario, so the principal OOM-resilience outcome is only partially satisfied.","limitations":["No captured test exhausted host-level memory: the hog was capped at 192 MiB by MemoryMax and the kernel explicitly reported a memory-cgroup OOM. Thus survival and victim selection under actual system-wide OOM remain unverified."]}
